= Biology
{wiki}

<Biology> studies living systems: how <biological cells> maintain themselves, how <organisms> develop and reproduce, and how <evolution> generates diversity. Its scales connect <molecules>, <genes>, <biological cells>, <organisms> and the interactions studied by <ecology>. These are complementary viewpoints: a <protein>'s chemistry can explain a cellular mechanism, while <evolution> explains why inherited variants of that mechanism persist. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/1-1-the-science-of-biology[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e: the science of biology].

Two organizing ideas make this immense subject navigable: shared cellular organization and descent with inherited modification. A <neuron> and a <gliding bacterium> differ dramatically in shape and behavior, yet both depend on a <cell membrane> and <metabolism>. Their differences become intelligible through specialization and <evolution>.

Keep observations separate from the <mathematical models> used to explain them. A measured <membrane potential> is evidence; an idealized <two-state ion-channel gating> equation proposes a mechanism. A <mathematical model> earns trust through predictions and comparisons with measurements. Its assumptions delimit its usefulness, and disagreement can reveal missing biology rather than a faulty measurement.

\Include[biology-author]
\Include[biology-bibliography]

= Life
{parent=Biology}

Systems capable of sustained self-maintenance and heritable <evolution>; definitions differ at boundary cases.

= Abiogenesis
{parent=Life}

Origin of <life> from nonliving chemical systems.

= Spontaneous generation
{parent=Abiogenesis}

Historical hypothesis that familiar organisms arise anew in organic preparations without pre-existing organisms of their kind; experiments on contaminated infusions must be distinguished from modern questions about the remote origin of life.

= Central dogma of molecular biology
{parent=Biology}
{wiki}

Sequence information may pass among <DNA> and <RNA> and from <RNA> to <protein>; the dogma excludes a general transfer from protein sequence back into nucleic-acid sequence. <Reverse transcription> does not contradict it.

= Toxicology
{parent=Biology}
{wiki}

= Chelation therapy
{parent=Toxicology}

= Arsenic poisoning
{parent=Toxicology}

= Antidote
{parent=Toxicology}

= Toxicologist
{parent=Toxicology}

= Oxalic acid poisoning
{parent=Toxicology}

Toxic effects of <oxalic acid>, including local corrosive injury, disturbed <calcium> balance and possible <acute kidney injury> with <calcium oxalate> deposition.<Oxalic Acid: International Chemical Safety Card 0529>[Short-term effects]{ref}<Acute Renal Failure Following Oxalic Acid Poisoning: A Case Report>[Renal biopsy]{ref}

= Antimuscarinic poisoning
{parent=Toxicology}

Poisoning involving <muscarinic receptor> blockade, with manifestations including <delirium>, dryness and <mydriasis>.<Anticholinergic Syndrome by the Royal Children’s Hospital Melbourne>[Background and characteristic signs]{ref}

= Alcohol intoxication
{parent=Toxicology}

= Drunkenness
{synonym}

= Lead poisoning
{parent=Toxicology}

= Carbon dioxide poisoning
{parent=Toxicology}

= Phosphorus poisoning
{parent=Toxicology}

The acute toxic syndrome caused especially by <white phosphorus>, distinguished from ordinary dietary <phosphate>.

= Gastric lavage
{parent=Toxicology}

= Stomach pump
{parent=Gastric lavage}

= Antimony poisoning
{parent=Toxicology}

= Opioid poisoning
{parent=Toxicology}

Toxic effects of <opioids>, especially <respiratory depression>; severe poisoning can require <artificial respiration> and <naloxone>.

= Opium poisoning
{parent=Opioid poisoning}

Poisoning by <opium>, distinguished historically from other causes of <coma>.

= Toxicological analysis
{parent=Toxicology}

= Arsenic detection
{parent=Toxicological analysis}

= Reinsch test
{parent=Arsenic detection}
{c}

A historical deposition test using <copper> in an acidified sample. Deposits require further characterization because substances other than <arsenic> can interfere.<On Arsenic as a Poison; Its Tests and Antidote by E. J. Shearman>[1844, pp.3–5]{ref}

= Marsh test
{parent=Arsenic detection}
{c}

A historical arsenic-separation and recognition procedure associated with <James Marsh>. Volatile <arsine> carries arsenic away from a sample matrix and yields a deposit; <stibine> can interfere.

= Strychnine
{parent=Toxicology}

A toxic substance associated with <strychnine poisoning>.

= Liquor Strychniae
{parent=Strychnine}
{c}

Historical pharmaceutical preparation; its composition and clinical use must be identified by edition. <The Essentials of Materia Medica and Therapeutics by Alfred Baring Garrod (1865)>[corresponding monograph and British preparation]{ref}

= Poison
{parent=Toxicology}

= Cyanide poisoning
{parent=Toxicology}

= Carbon monoxide poisoning
{parent=Toxicology}

= Strychnine poisoning
{parent=Toxicology}
{wiki}

= Toxicity
{parent=Toxicology}
{wiki}

= Agronomy
{parent=Biology}
{wiki}

Study of crops and their interaction with soils, climate and agricultural management. Field performance depends on these conditions as well as on the inherited characteristics examined in <plant breeding>.

= Lodging
{disambiguate=agriculture}
{parent=Agronomy}
{wiki}

Bending or displacement of crop stems from their upright position. Plant architecture, weather and growth conditions affect its occurrence and the resulting harvest losses.

= Agronomist
{parent=Agronomy}

