Absence of <menstruation>; it describes a sign with physiological, endocrine and anatomical explanations, rather than one disease.

= Secondary amenorrhoea
{parent=Amenorrhoea}

= Secondary amenorrhea
{synonym}

= Primary amenorrhoea
{parent=Amenorrhoea}

= Primary amenorrhea
{synonym}

= Faintness
{parent=Medicine}

A feeling of impending loss of consciousness. The word should not automatically be substituted for a demonstrated episode of <syncope>.

= Biopsy
{parent=Medicine}

= Skin biopsy
{parent=Biopsy}

= Rheumatology
{parent=Medicine}

= Rheumatism
{parent=Rheumatology}

Historical umbrella for painful musculoskeletal disorders, including conditions now separated as <acute rheumatic fever>, <rheumatoid arthritis> and <osteoarthritis>. It is not a single modern pathological diagnosis.

= Gout
{parent=Rheumatology}

An inflammatory <arthritis> associated with deposition of <urate crystals> in <anatomical joints>. Historical descriptions of <podagra> require interpretation rather than automatic assignment of every ancient patient to this modern category. <Gout by the NHS>[Symptoms, causes and diagnosis]{ref}

= Urate crystal
{parent=Gout}

A crystalline urate deposit involved in the inflammatory mechanism of <gout>. A historical description of <pain> does not by itself demonstrate such deposits. <Gout by the NHS>[Causes and diagnostic testing]{ref}

= Podagra
{parent=Gout}

<Gout> affecting the <foot>, particularly its big toe. The ancient word is retained in historical accounts of <gout>.

= Tophus
{parent=Gout}

= Tophi
{synonym}

= Chronic tophaceous gout
{parent=Gout}

= Acute gout
{parent=Gout}

= Osteoarthritis
{parent=Rheumatology}

= Rheumatoid arthritis
{parent=Rheumatology}

= Pannus
{parent=Rheumatoid arthritis}

