{parent=Pre-eclampsia}

= Heart failure
{parent=Medicine}

= Acute heart failure
{parent=Heart failure}

= Forensic medicine
{parent=Medicine}
{wiki}

= Forensic examination of newborns
{parent=Forensic medicine}

= Lung flotation test
{parent=Forensic examination of newborns}

A historical test of gas in the <lungs>, interpreted alongside decomposition, resuscitation and the whole examination. Flotation is not an infallible demonstration of <live birth> or drowning.

= Skeletal identification
{parent=Forensic medicine}

= Historical rape examination
{parent=Forensic medicine}

Historical medico-legal assessment of reported sexual violence. Medical observations do not independently determine consent; older resistance and virginity assumptions must be distinguished from current evidence.

= Putrefaction
{parent=Forensic medicine}

Decomposition of organic material, including changes to a body after death that can complicate assessment of <wound vitality>.

= Livor mortis
{parent=Forensic medicine}

Post-mortem pooling of <blood> in dependent body regions. Its appearance must be distinguished from a living response to injury.

= DNA profiling
{parent=Forensic medicine}
{c}

Comparison of variable regions of <DNA> for identity or relatedness investigations.

= Wound vitality
{parent=Forensic medicine}

= Bloodstain identification
{parent=Forensic medicine}

= Precipitin test
{parent=Bloodstain identification}

An <antibody>-based precipitation assay used, among other applications, to investigate the species of origin of a <bloodstain>.

= Haemin crystal test
{parent=Bloodstain identification}

A historical chemical test producing characteristic crystals from a <hemoglobin> derivative. It establishes neither human origin nor the donor's identity.

= Bloodstain
{parent=Bloodstain identification}

A deposit of <blood>, whose identification and source attribution are distinct forensic questions.

= Medical writer
{parent=Medicine}

= List of medical writers
{parent=Medical writer}

= Clinical observation
{parent=Medicine}

= Jaundice
{parent=Medicine}

Yellow discoloration associated with accumulation of <bilirubin>.

= Oedema
{parent=Medicine}

= Edema
{synonym}

Accumulation of excess fluid in <tissues>.

= Lymphoedema
{parent=Oedema}

= Anasarca
{parent=Oedema}

Extensive generalised interstitial fluid accumulation, commonly through combined circulatory, renal and protein-balance disturbances.

= Cerebral oedema
{parent=Oedema}

= Laryngeal oedema
{parent=Oedema}

= Pulmonary oedema
{parent=Oedema}

= History of medicine
{parent=Medicine}

Historical study of <medicine> ideas, practices and evidence. A historical indication for <treatment> must be distinguished from a present-day indication.

= Senile dotage
{parent=History of medicine}

Historical description of cognitive decline attributed to old age. It is not one precise modern <dementia> diagnosis.

= Mania in ancient Greek medicine
{parent=History of medicine}

= Ancient mania
{synonym}

Historical disease category, including in <Aretaeus of Cappadocia>, with an extent different from present-day <mania>. In I.6, persistent disturbance is distinguished from transient intoxication and <senile dotage>. <On the Causes and Signs of Chronic Diseases by Aretaeus of Cappadocia>[I.6]{ref}

= Fatty degeneration of the heart
{parent=History of medicine}

Historical pathological account of fatty change within damaged muscular fibres, distinguished from <adipose tissue> surrounding or extending between fibres. It is not a sufficient retrospective diagnosis of the cause of every <cardiac rupture>. <On Fatty Diseases of the Heart by Richard Quain>[1852 reproduction, pp. 120–126]{ref}

= Laryngismus stridulus
{parent=History of medicine}

A historical name for episodic spasmodic laryngeal obstruction, distinguished from sustained inflammatory or membranous <croup>.

= Pathological wind in ancient medicine
{parent=History of medicine}

Historical category used in explanations of bodily disturbance. In the <Celsus> cupping passage, wind and corrupted material should not be silently translated into a proven modern <infection> or a measured gas compartment. <On Medicine by Aulus Cornelius Celsus>[II.11]{ref}

= Cardiac affections of Aretaeus
{parent=History of medicine}

Historical category in <Aretaeus of Cappadocia> involving dangerous febrile illness and impending collapse. It should not be equated automatically with one present-day <heart> disorder or with modern <syncope>. <On the Treatment of Acute Diseases by Aretaeus of Cappadocia>[II.3, opening paragraphs]{ref}

= Critical days in ancient medicine
{parent=History of medicine}

Days on which an ancient prognostic framework expected decisive changes in a <disease>. Their use by <Aretaeus of Cappadocia> does not supply a fixed modern schedule for collapse, recovery or intervention. <On the Treatment of Acute Diseases by Aretaeus of Cappadocia>[II.3]{ref}

= Poultice
{parent=History of medicine}

A material applied locally to the body, often with warmth. In the assigned <Aretaeus of Cappadocia> passage it prepares the site for <wet cupping>; that historical purpose must be distinguished from a palliative application for comfort. <On the Treatment of Acute Diseases by Aretaeus of Cappadocia>[II.3]{ref}

= Cataplasma Fermenti
{parent=Poultice}
{c}

Historical formulation name; composition is identified by pharmacopoeial edition.

= Cataplasma Carbonis
{parent=Poultice}
{c}

Historical formulation name; composition is identified by pharmacopoeial edition.

= Cataplasma Lini
{parent=Poultice}
{c}

Historical formulation name; composition is identified by pharmacopoeial edition.

= Cupping
{parent=History of medicine}

Application of a <cupping vessel> to produce local suction. <Dry cupping> uses intact <skin>; <wet cupping> includes incisions and removal of <blood>. This procedural distinction does not itself establish therapeutic efficacy. <On Medicine by Aulus Cornelius Celsus>[II.11.1–6]{ref}

= Cupping vessel
{parent=Cupping}

A cup applied to the <skin> with reduced internal <pressure>. Ancient bronze and horn versions are described by <Celsus>. <On Medicine by Aulus Cornelius Celsus>[II.11.1–3]{ref}

= Ovariotomy
{parent=History of medicine}

Historical abdominal removal of an ovarian tumour. The indication, handling of a cyst and management of its pedicle differ from modern risk-stratified ovarian surgery.

= Emmenagogue
{parent=History of medicine}

A historical category of agents intended to provoke <menstruation>. The label neither establishes efficacy nor identifies the cause of <amenorrhoea>.

= Dropsy
{parent=History of medicine}

A historical term for abnormal accumulation of bodily fluid, overlapping with <oedema>. It describes an appearance or syndrome, not a single specific modern <disease>.

= Blistering agent
{parent=History of medicine}

A substance used historically to raise a blister as counterirritation. Historical use does not establish benefit or safety.

= Pythogenic theory of typhoid
{parent=History of medicine}

A historical claim that <typhoid fever> could originate from decomposing organic material, as opposed to transmission from an existing source of the specific <infection>.

= Miasma theory
{parent=History of medicine}

A historical theory attributing disease to harmful emanations. It must be distinguished from specific infectious and chemical causes.

= Serous drainage
{parent=History of medicine}

Historical therapeutic removal of serous fluid from an artificially raised <blister> or maintained superficial wound; loss of fluid alone does not establish clinical benefit.

= Counterirritation
{parent=History of medicine}

Application of an irritant to one part in the expectation of relieving symptoms elsewhere. Historical claims of remote cure require evidence beyond the demonstrated irritation.

= Rubefacient
{parent=Counterirritation}

A preparation causing redness of the skin, historically used for counterirritation.

= Materia medica
{parent=History of medicine}

Historical study of medicinal substances, their origins, preparation and effects; a predecessor and continuing descriptive component of <pharmacology> and <pharmacognosy>.

= Historical legitimacy and prolonged gestation
{parent=History of medicine}

= Historical English asylum admission
{parent=History of medicine}
{c}

= Medical historian
{parent=History of medicine}

= List of medical historians
{parent=Medical historian}

= Neil Pemberton
{parent=List of medical historians}
{c}

Research on the <history of medicine>. Institutional profile: https://research.manchester.ac.uk/en/persons/neil.pemberton

= Robert G. W. Kirk
{parent=List of medical historians}
{c}

Research on the <history of medicine>. Institutional profile: https://research.manchester.ac.uk/en/persons/robert.g.kirk

= Hippocratic Corpus
{parent=History of medicine}

Collection of ancient medical writings traditionally associated with <Hippocrates>. Association does not establish his personal authorship of every work.

= Numerical method in medicine
{parent=History of medicine}

Use of systematically recorded groups of cases and numerical comparisons to evaluate <treatment>, associated especially with <Pierre Charles Alexandre Louis>. Such comparisons need not be <randomized controlled trials> and remain vulnerable to <confounding>. <Research on the Effects of Bloodletting by Pierre Charles Alexandre Louis>[1835, pp. 70–76]{ref}

= Dry cupping
{parent=History of medicine}

Application of suction cups to intact <skin>, without the incisions used in <wet cupping>. It therefore does not itself constitute removal of <blood>. <On Medicine by Aulus Cornelius Celsus>[II.11.3]{ref}

= Buffy coat of historical bloodletting
{parent=History of medicine}

= Change-of-type theory of disease
{parent=History of medicine}

A nineteenth-century explanation of changing therapeutic practice: inflammatory <diseases> were said to have become less vigorous or more debilitating, requiring less depletion. It competed with explanations based on improved <pathology> and better evaluation of <treatment>. This is a historical theory, not a general modern law. <The Bloodletting Controversy (1858 review)>[pp. 1–3]{ref}

= Coeliac affection of Aretaeus
{parent=History of medicine}

The historical <chronic disease> described by <Aretaeus of Cappadocia> through wasting, <diarrhoea> and incomplete <digestion>. This symptomatic category is not automatically coextensive with modern <coeliac disease>. <On the Causes and Signs of Chronic Diseases by Aretaeus of Cappadocia>[II.7]{ref}

= Digestive heat
{parent=History of medicine}

Historical explanatory power thought to transform <food> into bodily nourishment by <digestion>. In <Aretaeus of Cappadocia>, weakness of this power explains the <coeliac affection of Aretaeus>; this is not the modern mechanism of <coeliac disease>. <On the Causes and Signs of Chronic Diseases by Aretaeus of Cappadocia>[II.7, opening paragraph]{ref}

= Humorism
{parent=History of medicine}

A historical explanatory system in which health and <disease> depend on the mixture and balance of bodily fluids. The four-fluid scheme includes <blood>, <humoral phlegm>, yellow <bile> and <black bile>. These categories are not a modern analysis of <blood> composition. <On the Nature of Man in the Hippocratic Corpus>[Selection 1, four distinct fluids]{ref}

= Humoral fluid
{parent=Humorism}

A bodily fluid considered within <humorism>; historical claims about its balance must be distinguished from current <physiology>.

= Black bile
{parent=Humorism}

One of the four fluids in classical <humorism>, distinct within that theory from yellow <bile>. Its historical category should not be treated as a demonstrated normal modern bodily fluid.

= Humoral phlegm
{parent=Humorism}

The cold fluid category called phlegm in historical <humorism>. It should not be equated without qualification with a present-day specimen of respiratory <mucus>.

= Plethora in historical medicine
{parent=Humorism}

= Bloodletting
{parent=History of medicine}

Deliberate removal of <blood> for a therapeutic purpose. Historical practices include <venesection>, <wet cupping> and <medicinal leeches>; their historical rationale must be separated from present-day indications for <venesection>.

= Medicinal leech
{parent=Bloodletting}

A <leech> used in <medicine>, historically to remove <blood> locally. The category is functional and does not identify one particular species.

= Wet cupping
{parent=Bloodletting}

Use of suction cups on incised <skin> to remove <blood>. <Celsus> describes bronze and horn cups and distinguishes this from <dry cupping>. <On Medicine by Aulus Cornelius Celsus>[II.11.1–6]{ref}

= Venesection
{parent=Bloodletting}

Removal of <blood> through a <vein>. The term can describe a historical intervention or a current <treatment> for a specific condition; the procedure alone does not establish its indication.

= Antecubital venesection
{parent=Venesection}

<Venesection> at the <elbow>. The ancient instruction to open a hollow <vein> here does not mean opening the <vena cava>.

= Thrombosis
{parent=Medicine}

= Embolus
{parent=Thrombosis}

Material carried in the circulation that can lodge and obstruct a vessel; a detached portion of a thrombus is one example.

= Thrombolysis
{parent=Thrombosis}

= Anticoagulation
{parent=Thrombosis}

= Low-molecular-weight heparin
{parent=Anticoagulation}

= Thrombophilia
{parent=Thrombosis}

= Hypercoagulability
{parent=Thrombosis}

= Thrombus
{parent=Thrombosis}

= Venous thrombosis
{parent=Thrombosis}

= Post-thrombotic syndrome
{parent=Venous thrombosis}

= Venous thromboembolism
{parent=Venous thrombosis}

= Deep vein thrombosis
{parent=Venous thrombosis}

= Phlegmasia cerulea dolens
{parent=Deep vein thrombosis}

Severe venous outflow obstruction producing a painful, swollen, blue limb and potentially threatening <perfusion>.

= Phlegmasia alba dolens
{parent=Deep vein thrombosis}

A historical clinical term for a pale, painful, swollen limb, especially after childbirth, associated with extensive <deep vein thrombosis>. It is distinct from blue, severely congested <phlegmasia cerulea dolens>.

= Obstetrics
{parent=Medicine}

= Uterine rupture
{parent=Obstetrics}

= Caesarean section
{parent=Obstetrics}
{c}

= Speculum examination
{parent=Obstetrics}

= Caesarean birth
{parent=Obstetrics}

= Controlled cord traction
{parent=Obstetrics}

A trained technique for placental delivery using traction on the <umbilical cord> with counterpressure supporting the <uterus>, under appropriate third-stage conditions.

= Third stage of labour
{parent=Obstetrics}

= Pregnancy
{parent=Obstetrics}

= Fetal movement
{parent=Pregnancy}

= Quickening
{parent=Pregnancy}

The mother's first perception of <fetal movement>; a symptom rather than an independent examiner's demonstration.

= Ballottement
{parent=Pregnancy}

Rebound felt after displacement of a mobile object in fluid. In a suitable obstetric setting it is a probable sign of <pregnancy>, not by itself proof of a living <fetus>.

= Hydatidiform mole
{parent=Pregnancy}

= Ectopic pregnancy
{parent=Pregnancy}

A <pregnancy> implanted outside the normal endometrial cavity; tubal rupture can cause severe internal <haemorrhage>.

= Miscarriage
{parent=Pregnancy}

= Gestational age
{parent=Pregnancy}

A dating measure conventionally reckoned from the first day of the last <menstruation>, rather than directly from observed <fertilization>.

= Decidua
{parent=Pregnancy}

= Placenta
{parent=Pregnancy}

= Placental tissue
{parent=Placenta}

= Umbilical cord
{parent=Placenta}

= Wharton's jelly
{c}
{parent=Umbilical cord}

= Umbilical vein
{parent=Umbilical cord}

= Umbilical artery
{parent=Umbilical cord}

= Succenturiate placental lobe
{parent=Placenta}

= Fetus
{parent=Pregnancy}

= Vernix caseosa
{parent=Fetus}

= Postpartum haemorrhage
{parent=Obstetrics}

= Placenta accreta spectrum
{parent=Obstetrics}

Abnormal adherence of the <placenta> to, or invasion into, the <myometrium>, associated with deficiency of the normal intervening <decidua>. Attempted forced separation may cause severe <haemorrhage>.

= Retained placenta
{parent=Obstetrics}

Failure to deliver the <placenta> after childbirth, through failure of separation, entrapment after separation, or abnormal attachment. Treatment depends on the mechanism and on <haemorrhage>.

= Antepartum haemorrhage
{parent=Obstetrics}

= Vasa praevia
{parent=Antepartum haemorrhage}

Fetal vessels in the membranes pass near or across the internal cervical opening without normal protection; rupture can rapidly cause fetal blood loss.

= Placental abruption
{parent=Antepartum haemorrhage}

= Placenta praevia
{parent=Antepartum haemorrhage}

= Ophthalmology
{parent=Medicine}

= Conjunctivitis
{parent=Ophthalmology}

= Interstitial keratitis
{parent=Ophthalmology}

= Refractive surgery
{parent=Ophthalmology}

= Refraction examination
{parent=Ophthalmology}

= Refraction test
{synonym}

= Crossed-cylinder test
{parent=Refraction examination}

A subjective <refraction examination> using equal positive and negative cylindrical powers at perpendicular axes to compare choices of correcting axis or power.

= Corneal topography
{parent=Refraction examination}

= Keratometry
{parent=Refraction examination}

= Retinoscopy
{parent=Refraction examination}

= Stenopaic slit
{parent=Refraction examination}

= Refractive error
{parent=Ophthalmology}

= Spectacles
{parent=Refractive error}

= Astigmatism
{disambiguate=eye}
{parent=Refractive error}
{wiki=Astigmatism}

= Ocular astigmatism
{synonym}

An ocular <refractive error> in which a point object cannot be brought to one sharp retinal focus by a single spherical correction. <Regular ocular astigmatism> has perpendicular principal meridians of unequal power; irregular departures require a more general description.<Astigmatism by the National Eye Institute>[Overview and correction]{ref}

= Irregular ocular astigmatism
{parent=Astigmatism (eye)}

= Regular ocular astigmatism
{parent=Astigmatism (eye)}

The two principal meridians have different powers and are perpendicular. A paraxial pencil forms two perpendicular line foci at distinct depths. A <cylindrical lens> or <toric lens> can supply the required meridional correction.<Physiology of Astigmatism by the American Academy of Ophthalmology>[Regular astigmatism]{ref}

= Urology
{parent=Medicine}

= Suprapubic catheterisation
{parent=Urology}

= Urethral trauma
{parent=Urology}

= Urethral stricture
{parent=Urology}

= Spasmodic urethral stricture
{parent=Urethral stricture}

Historical description of transient functional obstruction attributed to muscular <spasm>. It must be distinguished from a fixed fibrotic <urethral stricture> and from other causes of <urinary retention>.

= Bladder stone
{parent=Urology}

= Lithotrity
{parent=Urology}

Historical mechanical crushing of a <bladder stone> with an instrument introduced through the <urethra>. It must not be equated with later external shock-wave treatment.

= Lithotomy
{parent=Urology}

Operative extraction of a stone through an incision; the present historical discussion concerns a <bladder stone>.

= Urinary stone
{parent=Urology}

A stone formed in the urinary tract; symptoms and treatment depend on its composition, location, obstruction and associated infection.

= Calcium oxalate
{parent=Urinary stone}

= Tubular injury
{parent=Urology}

Injury affecting renal tubular cells, assessed with the clinical course, renal function and other findings.

= Interstitial nephritis
{parent=Urology}

Inflammation involving the renal interstitium.

= Urinary tract infection
{parent=Urology}

Infection involving the urinary tract; a contaminated sample or asymptomatic bacteriuria is not automatically symptomatic infection.

= Urinary retention
{parent=Urology}

= Hydrocele
{parent=Urology}

= Surgery
{parent=Medicine}

= Debridement
{parent=Surgery}

= Antisepsis
{parent=Surgery}

Measures directed against microorganisms on living tissue, distinct from merely covering a wound or deodorising a discharge.

= Catheter
{parent=Surgery}

= Cannula
{parent=Surgery}

A hollow tube introduced into the body; a historical tapping instrument leaves it in place after the <trocar> is withdrawn.

= Trocar
{parent=Surgery}

A pointed instrument used with a surrounding <cannula> to enter a fluid-containing cavity.

= Abdominal paracentesis
{parent=Surgery}

Withdrawal of fluid from the <peritoneum> with a needle or <catheter>, for diagnosis or relief of <ascites>.

= Mastectomy
{parent=Surgery}

= Asepsis
{parent=Surgery}

= Trocar and cannula
{parent=Surgery}

A penetrating instrument and associated tube used historically to enter and drain a cavity. Its historical use does not establish safety for modern ovarian-mass management.

= Laparoscopic cholecystectomy
{parent=Surgery}

= Ligature
{parent=Surgery}

= Colorectal surgery
{parent=Surgery}

= Rectal bleeding
{parent=Colorectal surgery}

= Anal stenosis
{parent=Colorectal surgery}

= Haemorrhoidectomy
{parent=Colorectal surgery}

= Haemorrhoid
{parent=Colorectal surgery}

= Haemorrhoidal disease
{synonym}

A vascular cushion of the <anal canal>; symptomatic bleeding, enlargement or prolapse constitutes haemorrhoidal disease.

= Infrared coagulation
{parent=Haemorrhoid}

= Sclerotherapy
{parent=Haemorrhoid}

= Rubber band ligation
{parent=Haemorrhoid}

= Hernia
{parent=Surgery}

= Internal hernia
{parent=Hernia}

Protrusion through an aperture within the abdominal cavity, rather than through its external wall.

= Diaphragmatic hernia
{parent=Hernia}

= Perineal hernia
{parent=Hernia}

= Sciatic hernia
{parent=Hernia}

= Obturator hernia
{parent=Hernia}

= Lumbar hernia
{parent=Hernia}

= Lateral ventral hernia
{parent=Hernia}

A lateral anterior-wall <hernia>, including the defect through the semilunar region lateral to the rectus sheath.

= Incisional hernia
{parent=Hernia}

= Epigastric hernia
{parent=Hernia}

= Umbilical hernia
{parent=Hernia}

= Femoral hernia
{parent=Hernia}

= Reduction en masse
{parent=Hernia}

Displacement of a hernia and its sac while a constriction remains, so apparent disappearance of the external swelling need not mean safe reduction.

= Richter hernia
{parent=Hernia}
{c}

= Partial-wall hernia
{synonym}

A hernia trapping only part of the bowel wall; severe vascular compromise may occur without complete obstruction of the lumen.

= Strangulated hernia
{parent=Hernia}

= Inguinal hernia
{parent=Hernia}

= Indirect inguinal hernia
{parent=Inguinal hernia}

= Direct inguinal hernia
{parent=Inguinal hernia}

= Scrotal hernia
{parent=Inguinal hernia}

= Orthopaedic surgery
{parent=Surgery}

= Sling
{parent=Orthopaedic surgery}

= Fracture
{parent=Orthopaedic surgery}

= Crack in a solid
{parent=Fracture}

= Crack
{synonym}

A crack is an internal separation surface, or a curve in a two-dimensional reduction, across which a displacement can have distinct traces. In the ideal traction-free model its two faces carry zero imposed traction. A crack tip or front terminates this separation and can produce a singular stress field. The <stress intensity factor> measures the leading singular amplitude, while the <energy release rate> measures the reduction in equilibrium potential energy per increment of crack extent.

= Joint dislocation
{parent=Orthopaedic surgery}

= Joint reduction
{parent=Joint dislocation}

= Shoulder dislocation
{parent=Joint dislocation}

= Luxatio erecta
{parent=Shoulder dislocation}

= Pathology
{parent=Medicine}

= Embolism
{parent=Pathology}

= Septic embolism
{parent=Embolism}

= Electrolyte imbalance
{parent=Pathology}

= Hypokalaemia
{parent=Electrolyte imbalance}

= Postmortem change
{parent=Pathology}

= Rigor mortis
{parent=Postmortem change}

Postmortem muscular stiffening associated with loss of sufficient <ATP> to maintain normal detachment of actin–myosin cross-bridges. It is distinct from freezing and heat coagulation.

= Fibroplastic growth
{parent=Pathology}

= Fibro-plastic growth
{synonym}

A historical morphological category of cellular, often spindle-celled growths suggesting fibrous differentiation. It does not map uniquely to one modern diagnosis or prove benign behaviour.

= Ulcer
{parent=Pathology}

= Aphthous ulcer
{parent=Pathology}

= Polyp
{parent=Pathology}

= Neuropathology
{parent=Pathology}

= Myelomalacia
{parent=Neuropathology}

Destructive softening of <spinal cord> tissue following injury such as <ischemia>, compression or <inflammation>.

= Encephalomalacia
{parent=Neuropathology}

Destructive softening of <brain> tissue, often following <cerebral infarction>. Gross consistency does not establish its cause.

= Periostitis
{parent=Pathology}

= Osteochondritis
{parent=Pathology}

= Hepatosplenomegaly
{parent=Pathology}

= Pathological anatomy
{parent=Pathology}

Correlation of anatomical changes with <disease> and <clinical observation>. Gross findings and clinical symptoms do not, on their own, necessarily identify a modern microscopic mechanism.

= Scirrhous hardening
{parent=Pathology}

= Scirrhous
{synonym}

A descriptive historical finding of hard, indurated tissue. Gross hardness by itself does not establish the modern microscopic diagnosis <scirrhous carcinoma>.

= Insomnia
{parent=Pathology}

= Sleeplessness
{synonym}

= Loss of appetite
{parent=Pathology}

= Arthritis
{parent=Pathology}

Inflammation of an <anatomical joint>. Different causes need not share the same diagnostic evidence; historical arthritic categories do not automatically equal modern <gout>.

= Stricture
{parent=Pathology}

= Cellular pathology
{parent=Pathology}

= Scrofulous diathesis
{parent=Pathology}

Historical constitutional predisposition invoked to explain scrofulous illness. It should be distinguished from the modern infectious cause and host risk factors of <tuberculosis>.

= Necrosis
{parent=Pathology}

= Liquefactive necrosis
{parent=Necrosis}

= Gangrene
{parent=Necrosis}

= Caseous necrosis
{parent=Necrosis}

= Caseation
{synonym}

A cheese-like pattern of <necrosis>, often associated with <tuberculous granulomas> but not uniquely diagnostic of <tuberculosis>. Architecture, microbiology and the surrounding viable <tissue> help establish the cause.

= Symmetrical disease
{parent=Pathology}

A <disease> whose lesions occur at corresponding sites on the two sides of the body, with similarity of form or extent. It is a distributional description rather than one causal diagnosis.

= Scrofulous material
{parent=Pathology}

Historical description of pathological products associated with scrofulous or tuberculous illness, often including caseous debris. It does not identify one chemical substance or prove deposition of a preformed material from <blood>.

= Cardiovascular disease
{parent=Pathology}

= Saphena varix
{parent=Cardiovascular disease}

A venous swelling near the termination of the great saphenous vein that can resemble a groin <hernia>.

= Respiratory disease
{parent=Pathology}

= Asthma
{parent=Respiratory disease}

= Respiratory failure
{parent=Respiratory disease}

= Hypoventilation
{parent=Respiratory failure}

= Acute kidney injury
{parent=Pathology}

= Rhabdomyolysis
{parent=Pathology}

Breakdown of skeletal <muscle> that can complicate intense generalized <muscle spasms>.

= Acidosis
{parent=Pathology}

= Hyperthermia
{parent=Pathology}

= Heatstroke
{parent=Hyperthermia}

A severe heat illness with neurological dysfunction, requiring rapid cooling and emergency treatment.

= Muscle spasm
{parent=Pathology}

= Risus sardonicus
{parent=Muscle spasm}

A characteristic fixed facial expression produced by contraction of facial <muscles>.

= Trismus
{parent=Muscle spasm}

Restricted jaw opening, including restriction caused by <muscle spasms>.

= Opisthotonus
{parent=Muscle spasm}

Marked backward arching caused by contraction of the back and neck <muscles>.

= Hemorrhage
{parent=Pathology}

Escape of <blood> from <blood vessels>.

= Wound
{parent=Pathology}

= Cytology
{parent=Pathology}

= Pus
{parent=Pathology}

= Cellular adaptation
{parent=Pathology}

= Renal pathology
{parent=Pathology}

= Renal disease
{parent=Renal pathology}

= Serum creatinine
{parent=Renal pathology}

= Glomerular filtration rate
{parent=Renal pathology}
{title2=GFR}

= Chronic kidney disease
{parent=Renal pathology}

= Fanconi syndrome
{parent=Renal pathology}
{c}

= Kidney failure
{parent=Renal pathology}

= Nephritis
{parent=Renal pathology}

= Tubulointerstitial nephritis
{parent=Nephritis}

= Bright's disease
{parent=Nephritis}
{c}

Historical category of <kidney> disease associated with <albuminuria> and often <oedema>, named after <Richard Bright>. It should not be equated with one modern microscopic diagnosis.<Bright's disease by the University of Leeds Library>[History]{ref}

= Glomerular disease
{parent=Renal pathology}

= Hypertensive kidney disease
{parent=Glomerular disease}

= Diabetic kidney disease
{parent=Glomerular disease}

= Pneumothorax
{parent=Pathology}

= Pleural effusion
{parent=Pathology}

= Pseudohypertrophy
{parent=Pathology}

Apparent enlargement in which increased fat or <connective tissue> can conceal loss of functioning <muscle>. Distinguish it from enlargement of working <muscle> fibres by <hypertrophy>.

= Asphyxia
{parent=Pathology}

= Raised intracranial pressure
{parent=Pathology}

= Peritonitis
{parent=Pathology}

= Congestion
{parent=Pathology}

= Gliosis
{parent=Pathology}

= Autopsy
{parent=Pathology}

= Haemorrhage
{parent=Pathology}

= Petechiae
{parent=Haemorrhage}

= Pulmonary haemorrhage
{parent=Haemorrhage}

= Intracranial haemorrhage
{parent=Haemorrhage}

= Atelectasis
{parent=Pathology}

Incomplete expansion or collapse of <lung> air spaces, usually with loss of volume. It can coexist with <pneumonia> but is not itself synonymous with inflammatory consolidation.<Atelectasis by Merck Manual Professional Edition>[Definition]{ref}

= Hyperplasia
{parent=Pathology}

An increase in the number of <biological cells> in a <tissue>, distinct from enlargement of existing <biological cells> by <hypertrophy>.<Cellular Pathology in Pathology: The Big Picture>[Hyperplasia]{ref}

= Hypertrophy
{parent=Pathology}

Enlargement of individual <biological cells>, distinct from increased cell number through <hyperplasia>. Historical numerical hypertrophy referred to what is now called <hyperplasia>.<Cellular Pathology by Rudolf Virchow (1860)>[Lecture III, pp. 93–95]{ref}

= Left ventricular hypertrophy
{parent=Hypertrophy}

= Cardiac arrest
{parent=Pathology}

= Asystole
{parent=Cardiac arrest}

= Sudden death
{parent=Pathology}

= Amyloidosis
{parent=Pathology}

A group of disorders involving deposition of misfolded <protein> fibrils in <tissue>. The name does not mean that the deposit is <starch>.<Amyloidosis by Merck Manual Professional Edition>[Introduction and Etiology]{ref}

= Transthyretin amyloidosis
{parent=Amyloidosis}

= AL amyloidosis
{parent=Amyloidosis}
{c}

= AA amyloidosis
{parent=Amyloidosis}
{c}

= Sago spleen
{parent=Amyloidosis}

A gross pattern of <amyloidosis> of the <spleen> in which translucent deposits in the <splenic white pulp> resemble sago grains. The pattern can accompany different forms of <amyloidosis>.<Hepatic amyloidosis: morphologic differences between systemic AL and AA types>[Abstract, sago pattern and limits of anatomical typing]{ref}

= Renal amyloidosis
{parent=Amyloidosis}

= Nephrotic syndrome
{parent=Pathology}

= Glomerulonephritis
{parent=Pathology}

= Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis
{parent=Glomerulonephritis}

An immune-mediated renal complication following group A <streptococcal infection>; it is distinct from direct bacterial invasion of the <kidney>.

= Proteinuria
{parent=Pathology}

Excess <protein> in <urine>. <Glomerular disease>, impaired <renal tubule> recovery and excess circulating small <proteins> produce different patterns; <albuminuria> names the albumin component.<Proteinuria in Clinical Methods>[Basic Science]{ref}

= Orthostatic proteinuria
{parent=Proteinuria}

= Albuminuria
{parent=Pathology}

Excess <serum albumin> excretion in <urine>, distinguished from <proteinuria> due mainly to other <proteins>.<Albuminuria: Albumin in the Urine by NIDDK>[What is albuminuria?]{ref}

= Nursing
{parent=Medicine}

= Nurse
{parent=Nursing}

= List of nurses
{parent=Nurse}

= Florence Nightingale
{parent=List of nurses}
{c}
{title2=1820–1910}

A nurse and reformer whose <Notes on Hospitals by Florence Nightingale> and <Notes on Nursing by Florence Nightingale> addressed the environment of care. External portrait: https://www.npg.org.uk/collections/search/portrait/mw56738/Florence-Nightingale[National Portrait Gallery, NPG x16139].

= Work by Florence Nightingale
{parent=Florence Nightingale}

= Lucy Bray
{parent=List of nurses}
{c}

Researcher whose work includes <Children's health literacy relating to a global pandemic (COVID-19): an international cross-sectional study by Lucy Bray and others>.

\Image[https://research.edgehill.ac.uk/files-asset/21080122/Lucy_Bray_photo_3_.jpg?f=jpg&w=160]
{width=160}
{title=Portrait of <Lucy Bray>}
{source=https://research.edgehill.ac.uk/en/persons/lucy-bray/}
{description=Photograph hosted by Edge Hill University; credits and usage information are on the linked source page.}

= Work by Lucy Bray
{parent=Lucy Bray}

= Ischemia
{parent=Medicine}

Insufficient blood supply for tissue needs. Its effects can change neural function and the relationship between activity and an observed imaging response.

= Myocardial ischaemia
{parent=Ischemia}

Inadequate <oxygen> delivery to the <myocardium> relative to its metabolic requirements.

= Myocardial infarction
{parent=Ischemia}

= Atrophy
{parent=Medicine}

Loss or reduction of tissue in an organ or region. Regional <brain> <atrophy> can accompany dysfunction but does not uniquely determine the affected computation or the direction of a functional-network change.

\Include[physician]{parent=Medicine}

= Medical researcher
{parent=Medicine}

= List of medical researchers
{parent=Medical researcher}

= Ted Kaptchuk
{parent=List of medical researchers}
{c}
{wiki}

= Ted J. Kaptchuk
{synonym}
{c}

Medical researcher studying <placebo effects> and the therapeutic encounter.

\Image[https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:Redirect/file/Ted_Kaptchuk_in_NCCIH_interview.png?width=260]
{title=Ted Kaptchuk}
{width=260}

https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ted_Kaptchuk_in_NCCIH_interview.png[Source: Wikimedia Commons].

= Work by Ted Kaptchuk
{parent=Ted Kaptchuk}

= Antonello Silvestri
{parent=List of medical researchers}
{c}

Medical researcher and first author of a clinical study of information-dependent side-effect reporting.

= Work by Antonello Silvestri
{parent=Antonello Silvestri}

= Adherence
{parent=Medicine}

The extent to which a person follows an agreed <treatment> plan. Understanding, tolerability, access and expectations can all affect <adherence>, so nonadherence is not a diagnosis of irrationality.

= National Institute for Health and Care Excellence
{parent=Medicine}
{c}

= NICE
{c}
{synonym}

A UK guidance-producing organization. The historical <OCD> treatment recommendations are cited as a clinical framework alongside primary trial evidence.

= Work by National Institute for Health and Care Excellence
{parent=National Institute for Health and Care Excellence}

= Public health
{parent=Medicine}

Study and action concerning health at the population level. Prevention research evaluates benefits, harms, reach and distribution of outcomes as well as efficacy among those who receive an intervention.

= Food safety
{parent=Public health}

= Meat inspection
{parent=Food safety}

= Whole-carcass condemnation
{parent=Meat inspection}

Exclusion of the entire carcass from food use. This differs from condemnation of an isolated diseased organ; the nature and distribution of disease, and the applicable jurisdiction and date, matter.<Current United States meat-inspection regulations, 9 CFR Part 311>[§§311.2 and 311.16, present comparative criteria]{ref}

= World Health Organization
{parent=Public health}
{c}

https://www.who.int

= Environmental health
{parent=Public health}
{wiki}

= Hospital bed space
{parent=Environmental health}

= Historical bread adulteration
{parent=Environmental health}

= Building ventilation
{parent=Environmental health}

= Air change rate
{parent=Building ventilation}
{title2=ACH}

= Air changes per hour
{synonym}

= Hospital ward ventilation
{parent=Building ventilation}

= Air pollution
{parent=Environmental health}
{wiki}

= Particulate matter
{parent=Air pollution}

= Air pollutant
{parent=Air pollution}

= Sanitation
{parent=Environmental health}
{wiki}

= Disinfection
{parent=Sanitation}

= Heat disinfection
{parent=Disinfection}

= Sewage irrigation
{parent=Sanitation}

Application of <sewage> to land; hydraulic suitability alone does not establish microbiological safety.

= Sewage
{parent=Sanitation}

= Dry-earth sanitation
{parent=Sanitation}

A historical excreta-management approach using dry earth in receptacles, together with organized removal.

= Water-carriage sewerage
{parent=Sanitation}

Removal of excreta and wastewater through drains and sewers using <water> as the carrying medium. Conveyance and safe final treatment are different requirements.

= Water quality
{parent=Public health}

Assessment of the chemical, physical and biological properties of <water> in relation to its intended use.

= Water hardness
{parent=Water quality}

The capacity of <water> to consume <soap>, chiefly because of dissolved <calcium> and <magnesium> ions. Quantitative results are commonly expressed as a <calcium carbonate> equivalent; this does not mean all the hardness originally existed as dissolved <calcium carbonate>.

= Soap titration of water hardness
{parent=Water hardness}

A historical quantitative test in which a standardized <soap> solution is added to a measured water sample until a specified persistent lather appears. A reagent blank accounts for the amount needed to form the lather itself.

= Albuminoid ammonia
{parent=Water quality}

The historical operational fraction of <ammonia> released by alkaline <permanganate> after preliminary removal of free <ammonia>. It is neither a specific albumen measurement nor necessarily all organic nitrogen.

= Sanitary survey
{parent=Water quality}

Investigation of a water supply and its surroundings to identify plausible contamination routes and failures of protective barriers.

= Microbiological water testing
{parent=Water quality}

Examination of <water> for specified <microorganisms> or microbial indicators using validated procedures. Chemical clarity alone cannot establish microbiological safety.

= Drinking water
{parent=Water quality}

<Water> intended for human consumption.

= Plumbosolvency
{parent=Drinking water}

The capacity of <water> to dissolve <lead> from materials it contacts; dissolved release should be distinguished from carriage of particles containing <lead>.<Lead in Drinking Water by the Drinking Water Inspectorate>[Sources and factors affecting concentration]{ref}

= Prevention of mental disorders
{parent=Public health}

Intervention intended to reduce new cases or relevant risks before the target disorder develops. A change in knowledge or symptoms is not automatically evidence of fewer first onsets; research must specify baseline diagnostic status and follow-up outcomes.

= Indicated prevention
{parent=Prevention of mental disorders}

Prevention targeting people showing early signs or markers without currently meeting the relevant diagnostic threshold. A study enrolling both previously affected and never-affected participants should distinguish recurrence from first onset.

= Selective prevention
{parent=Prevention of mental disorders}

Prevention targeting a group with elevated risk because of shared characteristics or experiences, without requiring each recipient to show early symptoms. Parental bereavement is one example of a selection criterion, not itself a diagnosis.

= Family Bereavement Program
{parent=Selective prevention}
{c}

A preventive program involving caregiver and youth groups after parental death, targeting parenting, <coping> and adjustment. Trials distinguish changes in symptoms and functioning from uniform prevention of every diagnosis.

= Universal prevention
{parent=Prevention of mental disorders}

Prevention offered to a whole specified population regardless of individually assessed risk. Universal delivery can produce different effects across subgroups and does not imply that every participant has equal risk.

= Penn Resiliency Program
{parent=Universal prevention}
{c}

= PRP
{c}
{synonym}

A cognitive-behavioral prevention program studied in school and other settings. A trial with a nonspecific-program comparison found heterogeneous school results and no overall average depressive-symptom benefit in the full sample; program identity alone does not guarantee prevention of disorder.<School-Based Prevention of Depressive Symptoms by Jane E. Gillham and others>[2007, three-arm trial and school variation]{ref}

= Good Behavior Game
{parent=Universal prevention}
{c}

A classroom behavior-management approach using rules, teams and contingent rewards. Preventive field trials test whether changing early classroom behavior affects later outcomes; results depend on population, implementation and follow-up, not only on the program's name.

= Epidemiology
{parent=Public health}

Study of the distribution and determinants of health-related outcomes in populations. <Incidence>, recurrence and <prevalence> answer different questions and should not be exchanged when evaluating prevention.

= Pandemic
{parent=Epidemiology}

An epidemic extending over a large geographic area or across countries. The term describes distribution and does not by itself specify the risk or severity for one individual.

= Psychiatric epidemiology
{parent=Epidemiology}

Study of the distribution, determinants and course of psychiatric outcomes in populations. Differences in symptom burden, first-onset incidence, recurrence and service-record diagnoses require different interpretations.

= Psychiatric epidemiologist
{parent=Psychiatric epidemiology}

= List of psychiatric epidemiologists
{parent=Psychiatric epidemiologist}

= E. Jane Costello
{parent=List of psychiatric epidemiologists}
{c}

A <psychiatric epidemiologist> who used an externally generated income change to investigate childhood psychiatric outcomes.<Relationships between poverty and psychopathology by E. Jane Costello and others>[2003, author and original research]{ref}

= Work by E. Jane Costello
{parent=E. Jane Costello}

= Protective factor
{parent=Epidemiology}

A characteristic associated with lower risk or a weaker effect of a risk exposure under specified conditions. Causal protection requires stronger evidence than an unadjusted association.

= Stress-buffering interaction
{parent=Protective factor}
{title2=$\partial\mathbb E[D\mid S,P]/\partial S=\beta_1+\beta_3P$}

A pattern in which a resource modifies the relationship between <adversity> and an adverse outcome. In the model $\mathbb E[D\mid S,P]=\beta_0+\beta_1S+\beta_2P+\beta_3SP$, a negative $\beta_3$ attenuates the <adversity> slope as the resource increases. Its interpretation is scale dependent, and an observational interaction is not automatically a causal buffering effect.

= Risk factor
{parent=Epidemiology}

A characteristic associated with increased probability of an outcome. Association can arise from causal influence, <confounding> or selection; being predictive does not automatically make a factor a modifiable causal target.

= Risk difference
{parent=Epidemiology}
{title2=$RD=p_1-p_0$}

The difference between two outcome <probabilities> over specified populations and follow-up periods. With intervention risk $p_1$ and comparison risk $p_0$, a beneficial reduction in an adverse outcome has $RD<0$ and absolute risk reduction $p_0-p_1$. A causal interpretation requires a suitable design or identification assumptions; a simple difference between selected observational groups is not automatically a treatment effect.

\Include[psychiatry]{parent=Medicine}

= Infectious disease
{parent=Medicine}

Disease caused by an infectious agent. Infection, detectable exposure and clinically damaging disease are distinct outcomes.

= Gonorrhoea
{parent=Infectious disease}

= Bacteraemia
{parent=Infectious disease}

Presence of <bacteria> in the <blood>; it is not synonymous with every systemic inflammatory illness or with all forms of <sepsis>.

= Metastatic infection
{parent=Infectious disease}

Infection established at a site distant from its primary focus through spread of microorganisms, commonly through the <blood>.

= Trichinellosis
{parent=Infectious disease}

= Trichinosis
{synonym}

A parasitic infection acquired through ingestion of infected meat containing <Trichinella> larvae.<DPDx: Trichinellosis>[Life cycle]{ref}

= Septicaemia
{parent=Infectious disease}

= Septicemia
{synonym}

A historical and clinical term associated with systemic infection involving the bloodstream; its use should not be treated as identical to every modern definition of <sepsis>.

= Pyemia
{parent=Infectious disease}

= Pyaemia
{synonym}

A historical clinical designation for disseminated suppurative infection with multiple secondary foci; distinguish the term from a modern universal definition of <sepsis>.

= Anthrax
{parent=Infectious disease}

= Chancroid
{parent=Infectious disease}

A genital ulcer disease distinct from <syphilis>.

= Chickenpox
{parent=Infectious disease}

= Varicella
{synonym}

The primary infection caused by varicella-zoster virus; distinct from <smallpox>.

= Smallpox
{parent=Infectious disease}

= Variola
{synonym}

The infection caused by variola virus. <Varioloid> describes modified disease, rather than a distinct infection.

= Varioloid
{parent=Smallpox}

Historical name for modified <smallpox>, especially following <vaccination>; distinct from <chickenpox>.

= Dracunculiasis
{parent=Infectious disease}

= Guinea-worm disease
{synonym}

= Waterborne disease
{parent=Infectious disease}

= Echinococcosis
{parent=Infectious disease}

= Syphilis
{parent=Infectious disease}

An infectious disease caused by <Treponema pallidum>.

= Neurosyphilis
{parent=Syphilis}

Involvement of the nervous system by <syphilis>; it is not confined to a single chronological stage.

= Tabes dorsalis
{parent=Neurosyphilis}

= General paresis
{parent=Neurosyphilis}

= General paralysis of the insane
{synonym}

A progressive neuropsychiatric syndrome historically described before its cause was securely established.

= Gummatous syphilis
{parent=Syphilis}

A late manifestation involving destructive inflammatory lesions called gummas.

= Chancre
{parent=Syphilis}

Primary lesion classically associated with acquired <syphilis>; appearance alone does not establish the diagnosis.

= Congenital syphilis
{parent=Syphilis}

Infection acquired before birth; an infant may initially lack visible signs. Historical “hereditary” terminology does not imply Mendelian inheritance.

= Encephalitis
{parent=Infectious disease}

= Meningitis
{parent=Infectious disease}

= Poliomyelitis
{parent=Infectious disease}

= Polio
{synonym}

A disease caused by <poliovirus>; paralytic disease is one possible consequence, not the result of every infection.

= Dysentery
{parent=Infectious disease}

= Otitis media
{parent=Infectious disease}

= Lymphadenitis
{parent=Infectious disease}

= Septic arthritis
{parent=Infectious disease}

= Faecal–oral transmission
{parent=Infectious disease}

Transmission in which infectious material from excreta reaches another person's mouth through such routes as food, <water> or hands.

= Malaria
{parent=Infectious disease}

= Cholera
{parent=Infectious disease}

= Tetanus
{parent=Infectious disease}

= Tetanus immune globulin
{parent=Tetanus}

= Tetanospasmin
{parent=Tetanus}

The neurotoxin associated with <tetanus>. Neutralizing unbound toxin differs from reversing toxin already acting at neural targets.

= Intestinal parasite
{parent=Infectious disease}

= Tuberculosis
{parent=Infectious disease}

= Urinary tuberculosis
{parent=Tuberculosis}

= Tubercle
{parent=Tuberculosis}

A historical pathological name for a small nodular lesion. Chemical examination can characterize the material in a lesion without establishing its infectious cause. The word does not name one pure <chemical compound>.

= Intestinal tuberculosis
{parent=Tuberculosis}

= Tuberculous granuloma
{parent=Tuberculosis}

= Typhus
{parent=Infectious disease}

= Streptococcal infection
{parent=Infectious disease}

= Group A streptococcal infection
{parent=Streptococcal infection}
{c}

= Fungal infection
{parent=Infectious disease}

= Candidiasis
{parent=Fungal infection}

An <infection> caused by members of <Candida>.

= Oral candidiasis
{parent=Candidiasis}

= Muguet
{c}
{synonym}

= Oral thrush
{synonym}

An opportunistic fungal <infection> of the oral mucosa, historically called <muguet>. White plaques are distinct from an <aphthous ulcer>.

= Ringworm
{parent=Fungal infection}

= Dermatophytosis
{synonym}

A dermatophyte <fungal infection> affecting keratin-containing tissue; the name does not imply a worm.

= Tinea barbae
{parent=Ringworm}

= Tinea corporis
{parent=Ringworm}

<Ringworm> of the body skin.

= Sepsis
{parent=Infectious disease}

= Gastroenteritis
{parent=Infectious disease}

= Scarlet fever
{parent=Infectious disease}

= Typhoid fever
{parent=Infectious disease}
{wiki}

= Typhoid diarrhoea
{parent=Typhoid fever}

Diarrhoea occurring during <typhoid fever>. The stool symptom does not measure the severity of systemic infection or exclude intestinal complications.

= Typhoid intestinal ulcer
{parent=Typhoid fever}

An ulcer arising with destruction of intestinal lymphoid <tissue> in <typhoid fever>. The classical larger lesion follows the longitudinal shape of a <Peyer's patch>.

= Taeniasis
{parent=Infectious disease}

Intestinal infection with an adult <Taenia>.

= Cysticercosis
{parent=Infectious disease}

Infection by tissue <cysticerci> of <Taenia solium>, acquired by ingestion of eggs.

= Pneumonia
{parent=Infectious disease}

An inflammatory <disease> of the <lung>. Nineteenth-century series concern historical clinical diagnoses; they should not be assigned a particular modern causative <pathogen> without evidence.

= Hepatization
{parent=Pneumonia}

The liver-like firmness of consolidated <lung> in classical descriptions of <pneumonia>. It is an appearance, not conversion into <liver> tissue.<Pneumonia Pathology in StatPearls>[Histopathology]{ref}

= Lobar pneumonia
{parent=Pneumonia}

= Bronchopneumonia
{parent=Pneumonia}

= Immune-related adverse event
{parent=Medicine}

Damage to normal tissues associated with therapeutic immune activation, including inflammatory complications of <immune checkpoint blockade>.

= Lymphodepletion
{parent=Medicine}

Reduction of a patient's lymphocyte populations, sometimes used to prepare for adoptive cell therapy. Its effects and toxicities are part of the full treatment regimen.

= Progression-free survival
{parent=Medicine}

Time from a defined starting point until disease progression or death under a specified clinical-trial definition. It differs from overall survival and response rate.

= Extravasation
{parent=Medicine}

Exit of circulating <tumour> cells through a vascular boundary into tissue, one step in a multistage metastatic process.

= Intravasation
{parent=Medicine}

Entry of <tumour> cells from tissue into a blood or lymphatic vessel, distinct from later survival or growth at a distant site.

= Premetastatic niche
{parent=Medicine}

A tissue environment altered before arrival of malignant cells in a way that can favour subsequent metastatic establishment. Conditioning must be distinguished from effects of <tumour> cells already present.

= Tumour microenvironment
{parent=Medicine}

The non-malignant cells, extracellular material, signals and physical conditions surrounding and interacting with a <tumour>. Its components can support or restrain progression depending on their state and context.

= Metastasis
{parent=Medicine}

= Metastases
{synonym}

The establishment of malignant growth at a site distant from the original <tumour>, requiring successful dissemination, survival and expansion. Circulating <tumour> cells alone do not establish that a <metastasis> has formed.

= Experimental metastasis assay
{parent=Metastasis}

An assay introducing <tumour> <biological cells> at a selected point of dissemination, for example by vascular injection. Its interpretation must state which preceding barriers were bypassed.

= Metastatic cascade
{parent=Metastasis}

A sequence of conditional transitions from a primary <tumour> to a distant growing lesion. A bypass assay tests only selected transitions, and failure at one stage need not identify the slowest step in every setting.

= Tumour dormancy
{parent=Metastasis}

Persistence without substantial net expansion under specified conditions. Cellular quiescence and a balance between proliferation and death are different possible mechanisms.

= Circulating tumour cell
{parent=Metastasis}

A <cancer> <biological cell> detected in circulation. Detection measures one part of dissemination rather than total metastatic success.

= Disseminated tumour cell
{parent=Metastasis}

A <cancer> <biological cell> found away from the primary site. Its presence does not prove that it will generate a clinically significant distant colony.

= Metastatic colonization
{parent=Metastasis}

Establishment and sustained expansion at a distant site. A disseminated <biological cell> can survive or remain dormant without completing this stage.

= Carcinoma
{parent=Medicine}

A malignant <tumour> of epithelial origin. Its cells can change phenotype without ceasing to belong to their original malignant lineage.

= Colorectal cancer
{parent=Carcinoma}

= Basal cell carcinoma
{parent=Carcinoma}

= Rodent ulcer
{parent=Basal cell carcinoma}

Historical name particularly associated with locally destructive <basal cell carcinoma>.

= Squamous cell carcinoma
{parent=Carcinoma}

= Epithelioma
{parent=Carcinoma}

= Mucinous carcinoma
{parent=Carcinoma}

= Scirrhous carcinoma
{parent=Carcinoma}

= Tumour
{parent=Medicine}

An abnormal tissue mass. The term includes benign as well as malignant growths, so <tumour> and <cancer> are not always interchangeable.

= Leiomyoma
{parent=Tumour}

A benign <smooth muscle> tumour; a <uterine fibroid> is the familiar uterine form.

= Testicular tumour
{parent=Tumour}

= Lipoma
{parent=Tumour}

= Fibroma
{parent=Tumour}

= Brain tumour
{parent=Tumour}

= Recurrent fibroid tumour
{parent=Tumour}

A nineteenth-century descriptive category of recurrent fibrous or fibroplastic growths, some showing destructive behaviour or <metastasis>. It is not equivalent to a benign uterine fibroid.

= Oncology
{parent=Medicine}
{wiki}

= Cancer
{parent=Oncology}
{wiki}

= Endocrine therapy
{parent=Cancer}

= Breast cancer
{parent=Cancer}

= Breast cancer recurrence
{parent=Breast cancer}

Return of <breast cancer> after treatment, distinguished as local, regional or distant disease.

= Ovarian cancer
{parent=Cancer}

= Lung cancer
{parent=Cancer}

= Melanotic tumour
{parent=Cancer}

A historical gross category for a pigmented <tumour>; it is not automatically synonymous with <melanoma>.

= Tumour stage
{parent=Cancer}

= Tumour grade
{parent=Cancer}

= Germ cell tumour
{parent=Cancer}

= Leukaemia
{parent=Cancer}

= Leucocythaemia
{parent=Leukaemia}

= Leucocythemia
{synonym}

Historical term for an excess of white blood corpuscles, especially the persistent organ-associated disorders subsequently classified as <leukaemia>. The old term does not make every raised white count malignant.

= Lymphoma
{parent=Cancer}

= Melanoma
{parent=Cancer}

= Multiple myeloma
{parent=Cancer}

= Sarcoma
{parent=Cancer}

= Fibrosarcoma
{parent=Sarcoma}

= Colloid cancer
{parent=Cancer}

Historical designation for gelatinous malignant tissue, often associated with <mucin>. It is not a complete modern classification.

= Encephaloid cancer
{parent=Cancer}

Historical soft, brain-like malignant growth. The gross appearance does not specify one modern diagnosis in <histology>.

= Chronic lymphocytic leukaemia
{parent=Cancer}

A mature B-cell malignancy characterized by accumulation of a clonal population. Its initiation, indolent expansion and progression are distinct biological endpoints.

= Tumour latency
{parent=Cancer}

The interval between a defined starting event and detection of <tumour> development under a specified assay. Detection threshold and competing causes of death affect interpretation.

= Tumour maintenance
{parent=Cancer}

Continued survival or growth of established malignant disease. A <gene> required before initiation need not remain necessary after additional alterations accumulate.

= Autochthonous tumour model
{parent=Cancer}

A model in which <tumours> arise within the relevant host tissue rather than being introduced as an established graft. Defined engineered initiation still differs from a full human exposure history.

= Humanized mouse
{parent=Cancer}

A mouse modified to contain selected human biological components, such as immune <biological cells>. Humanization is partial and does not automatically reproduce all human immune interactions.

= Syngeneic tumour model
{parent=Cancer}

A tumour-graft model using a genetically compatible host and donor, enabling investigation in an immune-competent setting. It generally uses mouse rather than human <tumour> <biological cells>.

= Genetically engineered mouse model
{parent=Cancer}

A mouse model in which defined genetic changes are introduced to test disease mechanisms. Tissue, timing, expression level and <genetic background> constrain its relationship to human <cancer>.

= Immune evasion
{parent=Cancer}

Avoidance or suppression of immune-mediated elimination. Normal immune-regulatory mechanisms can be repurposed in a <tumour> context.

= Tumour clonality
{parent=Cancer}

The organization of a <tumour> into related lineages sharing inherited changes. A clonal alteration need not be a causal driver because passengers can hitchhike during expansion.

= Tumour purity
{parent=Cancer}

The proportion of a sampled <tumour> preparation contributed by malignant <biological cells>. It affects interpretation of copy number, variant fractions and expression measurements.

= Cancer dependency
{parent=Cancer}

A requirement for a particular activity in maintenance of a <cancer> under specified conditions. A dependency is not automatically an initiating driver or a selective therapeutic target.

= Oncogene addiction
{parent=Cancer dependency}

Strong dependence of an established <tumour> on an oncogenic activity. Dependence can change as the <tumour> acquires cooperating or bypass alterations.

= Chronic myeloid leukaemia
{parent=Cancer}

A myeloid malignancy classically associated with the BCR-ABL fusion. Dependence on a defined kinase driver provides a mechanistic basis for targeted inhibition.

= Cancer invasion
{parent=Cancer}

<Cancer invasion> is penetration of cancer cells into surrounding tissue across the normal boundaries of their compartment. It is distinct from <cell proliferation> and from completion of distant metastatic colonization.

= Collective cancer invasion
{parent=Cancer invasion}

= Collective invasion
{synonym}

Invasion by connected or coordinated groups of <cancer> <biological cells> rather than only isolated migrants. Leader and follower <biological cells> can perform different tasks.<Collective invasion in breast cancer requires a conserved basal epithelial program by Kevin J. Cheung and others>[Functional leader-cell experiments]{ref}

= Invadopodium
{parent=Cancer invasion}

= Invadopodia
{synonym}

An actin-rich cellular protrusion associated with localized <extracellular matrix> degradation during invasion. Formation, matrix digestion and whole-cell invasion are related but distinct experimental endpoints.

\Include[pharmacology]{parent=Medicine}

= Infection
{parent=Medicine}
{wiki}

= Infected
{synonym}

= Infection case
{parent=Infection}

An <infection case> is one affected individual counted in an outbreak model. A count of eventual cases can include exposures that have occurred but whose delayed transfer into the infective compartment is still pending.

= Bacterial infection
{parent=Infection}

An <infection> caused by <bacteria>.

An <infection> involves invasion and multiplication of an infectious agent in a <host>. <Bacteria> are one possible cause; an agent capable of causing <disease> is a <pathogen>. The National Cancer Institute defines infection through invasion and growth in the body. https://www.cancer.gov/publications/dictionaries/cancer-terms/def/infection[NCI Dictionary: infection]{ref}.

In a <SIR model>, acquiring <infection> moves a member of the <susceptible compartment> into the <infectious compartment>. That immediate transition is a model assumption: the <SEIR model> instead includes an exposed stage before infectiousness. Specify these states when translating a biological process into <compartmental models (epidemiology)>.

= Diphtheria
{parent=Bacterial infection}

= Carbuncle
{parent=Bacterial infection}

A deep, coalescent infection of adjacent hair follicles, distinguished from a single superficial pustule. The label alone does not identify the organism or establish an actual patient’s findings.

= Cellulitis
{parent=Bacterial infection}

= Immunity
{disambiguate=medical}
{parent=Medicine}

= Immunity
{synonym}

In infection modelling, <immunity> is protection against infection or disease, with its strength and duration represented by the chosen <host> states. A <SIR model> has a permanently protected recovered state, whereas a <SIS model> has no lasting post-recovery protection.

= Complement system
{parent=Immunity (medical)}
{wiki}

The <complement system> comprises immune <proteins>; its components provide an interaction partner for <Fc fragments>.

= Isolation
{disambiguate=health care}
{parent=Medicine}
{wiki}

<Isolation (health care)> separates recognized infected or ill individuals from susceptible contacts to limit transmission. <Quarantine> instead concerns exposed individuals who may or may not be infected.

= Psychoactive drug
{parent=Medicine}
{wiki}

A psychoactive drug is a substance that changes mental activity, mood or perception. In population <causal inference>, its per-user adverse-event risk and its total population harm are distinct: the latter also depends on how many people use it, for how long, and on substitution between substances.

= Mephedrone
{parent=Psychoactive drug}
{wiki}

Mephedrone is 4-methylmethcathinone, a synthetic stimulant <psychoactive drug>. The chemical identity is described in https://www.euda.europa.eu/publications/drug-profiles/synthetic-cathinones_en[the EUDA synthetic-cathinone profile]. In <causal inference>, an association between increased use and fewer deaths involving another drug does not itself establish substitution: changes in use, population composition and ascertainment must be distinguished.

= MDMA
{c}
{parent=Psychoactive drug}
{wiki}

= Ecstasy
{synonym}

MDMA is 3,4-methylenedioxymethamphetamine, a <psychoactive drug> commonly called ecstasy. A product's street label does not establish its measured chemical contents. Epidemiological comparisons should distinguish single-substance outcomes from outcomes attributed to several drugs.

= Cocaine
{parent=Psychoactive drug}
{wiki}

Cocaine is a stimulant <psychoactive drug>. Comparisons of its population harm require exposure denominators and consistent definitions of drug-related outcomes, rather than death counts alone.

= Informed consent
{parent=Medicine}
{wiki}

Informed consent is voluntary agreement after receiving and understanding relevant information about an intervention or participation in research. In a <clinical trial>, the participant is informed about the study procedures, potential benefits and risks, alternatives and the option to decline or withdraw. Consent precedes <randomization>.

= Risk communication
{parent=Informed consent}

Communicating the nature, <probability> and uncertainty of benefits and harms in a form suitable for the decision. Absolute frequencies with explicit denominators, balanced framing and plans for responding to symptoms support comprehension without suppressing material information.

= Hematopoietic stem cell transplantation
{parent=Medicine}
{wiki}

= HSCT
{synonym}
{c}

= Haematopoietic stem cell transplantation
{synonym}

\Include[neurology]{parent=Medicine}

= Disease
{parent=Medicine}
{wiki}

= Liver disease
{parent=Disease}

= Cholestasis
{parent=Liver disease}

Impaired flow or secretion of <bile>; it can arise within the <liver> or from extrahepatic obstruction.

= Hepatitis
{parent=Liver disease}

= Obesity
{parent=Disease}

= Uraemia
{parent=Disease}

= Polycythaemia vera
{parent=Disease}

A <disease> involving excessive production of <red blood cells>, for which <venesection> is one established <treatment>. <Erythrocytosis by the NHS>[Treatment, Venesection]{ref}

= Haemochromatosis
{parent=Disease}

A condition involving excessive accumulation of <iron>. <Venesection> removes <iron> within <red blood cells> and stimulates use of further <iron> in their replacement. <Haemochromatosis treatment by the NHS>[Venesection]{ref}

= Anaemia
{parent=Disease}

= Nosebleed
{parent=Disease}

Loss of <blood> through the nose; a descriptive event, not by itself identification of a <disease>.

= Diarrhoea
{parent=Disease}

Passage of loose or watery material from the <intestine>; a description of a symptom rather than identification of its cause.

= Malabsorption
{parent=Disease}

= Coeliac disease
{parent=Disease}

A <chronic disease> in which an <immune response> triggered by <gluten> damages the <small intestine>. The modern definition must not simply be read back into the <coeliac affection of Aretaeus>. <Celiac Disease by the National Institute of Diabetes and Digestive and Kidney Diseases>[Definition and Facts]{ref}

= Acute disease
{parent=Disease}

A <disease> with a relatively rapid onset or short course, in contrast to a <chronic disease>. Ancient authors used their own classifications; their boundaries need not match modern ones.

= COVID-19
{parent=Disease}
{c}

An infectious <disease> caused by <SARS-CoV-2> and central to the <pandemic> discussed in this examination. A study of children's explanations of <COVID-19> measures their understanding rather than the biological risk of a particular activity.

= Hepatic steatosis
{parent=Disease}

= American foulbrood
{parent=Disease}
{c}

A bacterial <disease> of honeybee brood caused by <Paenibacillus larvae>. Colony-level <disease> outcomes are distinct from an individual worker’s immune activity.

= Rasopathy
{parent=Disease}
{c}

A developmental syndrome associated with dysregulation of <Ras>-controlled signaling. A broad phosphoprotein pattern alone does not establish diagnosis or identify a causal mutation.

= Diabetes
{parent=Disease}
{wiki}

= Diabetic ketoacidosis
{parent=Diabetes}

A metabolic complication involving excessive <ketogenesis> and <acidosis>, generally in the setting of severe inadequate <insulin> action.

= Type 1 diabetes
{parent=Diabetes}
{wiki}

= Type 2 diabetes
{parent=Diabetes}
{wiki}

= Insulin resistance
{parent=Diabetes}
{wiki}

A reduced biological response to <insulin>; it is a property of response, not simply a low <insulin> <concentration>. The <glucose clamp technique> helps separate these quantities.

= Prion disease
{parent=Disease}

A prion disease is associated with self-propagating abnormal folding of a <protein>. Its epidemiological interpretation requires distinguishing exposure, infection, clinical onset and diagnostic detection.

= Variant Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease
{parent=Prion disease}

= vCJD
{c}
{synonym}

A human <prion disease> associated with exposure to the <BSE> agent. Exposure totals and clinical-onset totals measure different stages of the disease process; <incubation periods> and ascertainment affect their relationship.

= Scrapie
{parent=Prion disease}

A <prion disease> of sheep and goats. A <scrapie> test result and a <BSE> diagnosis are distinct outcomes, even when the clinical appearances overlap.

= Bovine spongiform encephalopathy
{parent=Prion disease}

= BSE
{c}
{synonym}

A <prion disease> of cattle. A positive surveillance test is an observed diagnostic outcome, whose frequency need not equal true infection <prevalence> unless test performance and sampling are accounted for.

= Myelodysplastic syndrome
{parent=Disease}
{wiki}

= MDS
{synonym}
{c}

= Chronic disease
{parent=Disease}

A chronic disease persists over an extended period. A <continuous-time multi-state model> can represent its progression through severity states, but long duration alone does not imply irreversible progression or justify ignoring state misclassification.

= Immunology
{parent=Medicine}
{wiki}

= Immune system
{parent=Immunology}

= Vaccine
{parent=Immunology}
{wiki}

= Vaccination
{parent=Vaccine}
{wiki}

The administration of a <vaccine>.

= Vaccination intention
{parent=Vaccination}

= Vaccine willingness
{synonym}

A person's reported intention or willingness to receive a vaccine under the conditions of a question. It is distinct from actual <vaccination status>. The response depends on the question, time and circumstances; an intention score does not directly measure later receipt or a medical effect.

= Vaccination status
{parent=Vaccination}

A record of which <vaccinations> a person has received at a specified time. A binary <categorical variable> may collapse this into two recorded categories; the categories alone do not specify number of doses, timing, protective <immunity> or willingness to receive a <vaccine>.

= Immune-competent host
{parent=Immunology}

= Immune response
{parent=Immunology}

Cellular and molecular changes following recognition of a challenge. Increased activation can reflect increased exposure or damage, so its magnitude is not interchangeable with protective capacity.

= Innate immunity
{parent=Immunology}

= Adaptive immunity
{parent=Immunology}

= Inflammation
{parent=Immunology}

= Myelitis
{parent=Inflammation}

= Pleurisy
{parent=Inflammation}

= Pleuritis
{synonym}

Inflammation of the pleura.

= Synovitis
{parent=Inflammation}

= Abscess
{parent=Inflammation}

A localized collection of <pus>.

= Retropharyngeal abscess
{parent=Abscess}

= Peritonsillar abscess
{parent=Abscess}

= Granuloma
{parent=Inflammation}

= Exudate
{parent=Inflammation}

= Inflammatory exudate
{parent=Inflammation}

= Inflammatory exudation
{synonym}

Material that leaves the <blood vessels> during <inflammation>. Historical theories of its formation and removal should be distinguished from current <pathology>.

= Fibrinous exudate
{parent=Inflammatory exudate}

An <inflammatory exudate> rich in <fibrin>.

= Immune challenge
{parent=Immunology}

Experimental manipulation intended to stimulate an <immune response>. A nonreplicating inflammatory stimulus separates some host responses from effects of a live <pathogen>, but need not reproduce natural <infection>.

= Disease tolerance
{parent=Immunology}

Reduction of health or fitness damage for a given <pathogen> burden. Low symptoms alone cannot distinguish tolerance from successful <disease resistance>.

= Disease resistance
{parent=Immunology}

Reduction of <pathogen> burden through prevention or control of <infection>. It differs from <disease tolerance>, which reduces damage at a given burden.

= Psychoneuroimmunology
{parent=Immunology}

Study of interactions among <animal behaviour>, the <nervous system> and <immunity>. Bidirectional mechanisms require interventions or temporal evidence beyond a cross-sectional association.

= Social immunity
{parent=Immunology}

Collective protection against <pathogens> arising through interactions among individuals, including <hygienic behaviour>. A colony-level reduction in <infection> need not be increased cellular <immunity> in every worker.

= MHC class II
{parent=Immunology}
{c}

An antigen-presentation system displaying peptides to appropriate CD4 T cells. Its usual source and processing route differ from the intracellular-protein sampling pathway of MHC class I.

= MHC class I
{parent=Immunology}
{c}

A major histocompatibility complex presentation system displaying intracellularly derived peptides to appropriate <T cells>. Presentation and direct CAR recognition have different requirements.

= T-cell exhaustion
{parent=Immunology}
{c}

An altered T-cell functional and regulatory state associated with persistent stimulation. Blocking one inhibitory receptor need not reverse every component of the state.

= HLA
{parent=Immunology}
{c}

Human leukocyte antigen, the human MHC system. Particular <HLA> molecules restrict which peptides a conventional T-cell receptor can recognise.

= Cancer vaccine
{parent=Immunology}

An intervention intended to generate or strengthen immune recognition of tumour-associated targets. Successful priming does not guarantee entry or function within a <tumour>.

= Adoptive T-cell therapy
{parent=Immunology}

Transfer of prepared <T cells> to a patient to supply or amplify a therapeutic immune response, including expanded tumour-derived or genetically engineered populations.

= Immune checkpoint blockade
{parent=Immunology}

Treatment that interferes with inhibitory immune regulatory interactions, such as <PD-1>/<PD-L1> or CTLA-4-associated restraint. It requires a usable antitumour recognition and effector system.

= Antigen presentation
{parent=Immunology}

Display of antigen-derived material for immune recognition, including peptide display by MHC molecules to <T-cell receptors>. Processing, expression and recognition are distinct requirements.

= Cross-presentation
{parent=Antigen presentation}

Presentation of externally acquired antigen on MHC class I. Dendritic cells can thereby prime antiviral CD8 responses without themselves being productively infected.

= MHC restriction
{parent=Antigen presentation}
{c}

Dependence of a T-cell response on both a recognized peptide and a compatible presenting MHC molecule. Infected matched and mismatched targets distinguish it from recognition of viral infection alone.

= Tapasin
{parent=Antigen presentation}

A component of the MHC-class-I peptide-loading machinery. It helps connect peptide supply and selection to stable class-I display.

= TAP
{parent=Antigen presentation}
{c}

Transporter associated with antigen processing, supplying peptides to the endoplasmic reticulum for MHC-class-I loading. Peptide-pulsing experiments can bypass this transport step.

= Immunologist
{parent=Immunology}

= List of immunologists
{parent=Immunologist}

= Anthony J. Filiano
{parent=List of immunologists}
{c}

= Tim Lämmermann
{parent=List of immunologists}
{c}

= Work by Tim Lämmermann
{parent=Tim Lämmermann}

= Gerald Edelman
{c}
{parent=List of immunologists}
{wiki}

= Gerald Maurice Edelman
{c}
{synonym}

= Gerald M. Edelman
{c}
{synonym}

Identity source: https://digitalcommons.rockefeller.edu/faculty-members/29/

Full-name memorial: https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4132271/

